Ticket #4471 is not one date bug. It contains **two distinct problems**, so file one engineering
issue for each: the shared parser problem and the `booking-v3` form-hint problem.

## Count the reports by cause

The export contains overlapping pages. Deduplicate by report ID before counting, then classify each
report from its tenant's configured locale and form, plus the reporting channel. `meta.intakeTag` is
only intake's guess and does not determine the cause. The table computes the report counts from the
export.

<FindingsTable ticket={4471} />

## Problems to track separately

### Shared date parser

The shared parser reads every date month-first. Tenants whose locale writes the day first therefore
get dates flipped. This affects tenants across app, email, and form channels. Track the parser defect
in [GitHub issue #1](https://github.com/smigacz/s01e03/issues/1); its report count is in the table above.

### `booking-v3` date hint

For day-first tenants, `booking-v3` displays `MM/DD/YYYY`. People follow that hint, while the
tenant's form interprets the date using its own day-first convention. These reports come from the
form channel and tenants running `booking-v3`. Track this form-hint defect in [GitHub issue #2]
(https://github.com/smigacz/s01e03/issues/2); its report count is in the table above.

## Why the original merge hid the split

The accepted triage rule merges reports on matching subject text alone. Different wording for the
same cause can miss that rule, and similar date wording can hide different causes. The desk's
`intakeTag` also disagrees with the cause for some reports; use the tenant locale, form version, and
channel instead. Reports noted as one-day moves remain classified by their tenant's configuration;
they are not a third cause.

